WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.218
Condition variables are coordination tools, not just notification channels.

2
00:00:03.218 --> 00:00:12.155
They work with a mutex and a shared condition, such as a queue becoming non-empty, a shutdown flag being set, or capacity becoming available.

3
00:00:12.155 --> 00:00:14.300
A wait should use a predicate.

4
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:20.377
The predicate describes the actual condition that allows progress and is checked while holding the correct lock.

5
00:00:20.377 --> 00:00:26.097
This pattern handles spurious wakeups and also handles notifications that happen before a thread starts waiting.

6
00:00:26.097 --> 00:00:28.600
Producer-consumer queues are a common example.

7
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:33.247
The queue contents, size, capacity policy, and shutdown state should be protected consistently.

8
00:00:33.247 --> 00:00:41.827
A consumer should not wake merely because a notification occurred; it should wake because the predicate says work is available or shutdown has been

9
00:00:41.827 --> 00:00:42.185
requested.

10
00:00:42.185 --> 00:00:44.330
Avoid busy waiting and timing assumptions.

11
00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:52.552
Sleeping for a short duration and hoping another thread has finished is fragile under load, on slower systems, during debugging, and in virtualized

12
00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:52.910
environments.

13
00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:55.770
Explicit coordination is more reviewable and more reliable.

14
00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.558
Bounded queues can support reliability.

15
00:00:57.558 --> 00:01:04.707
If input can arrive faster than workers process it, an unbounded queue can turn a temporary spike into memory pressure.

16
00:01:04.707 --> 00:01:09.355
A capacity policy gives the system a predictable behavior when resources are constrained.

17
00:01:09.355 --> 00:01:10.428
Notification discipline matters.

18
00:01:10.428 --> 00:01:16.148
Decide when to notify one waiter, when to notify all, and how shutdown signals are delivered.

19
00:01:16.148 --> 00:01:20.080
A shutdown path should wake threads that might otherwise wait forever.

20
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:26.872
Review coordination code by identifying the mutex, the predicate, the shared state, the notification points, and the shutdown behavior.

21
00:01:26.872 --> 00:01:31.520
If those pieces are clear, condition-variable code becomes much easier to maintain.
